    <body>
      <sec id="article-141167.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Microspherophakia is a rare congenital entity characterized by a biconvex spherical lens with a reduced equatorial diameter, primarily due to developmental anomalies of the zonules. The clinical manifestations can range from asymptomatic to high lenticular myopia, acute angle-closure, or chronic glaucoma. The diagnosis is based on meticulous clinical evaluation and a dilated-pupil examination, during which the equatorial lens margins are readily visible. Anterior segment optical coherence tomography or ultrasound biomicroscopy aids in assessing lens curvature, thickness, and angle structures, highlighting crowding due to the spherical lenticular shape.&#x000a0;A high index of clinical suspicion, careful examination, regular follow-ups to correct refractive error or plan surgical intervention, and regular postoperative follow-ups, with monitoring of intraocular pressure and the fundus for glaucomatous damage, form the basis of successful management. This review describes the etiology, predisposing risk factors, variable clinical manifestations, investigations, imaging modalities, treatment protocols, and postoperative care for patients with microspherophakia, as described by an interprofessional team.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the characteristic clinical features of microspherophakia, including lenticular myopia, phacodonesis, and lens subluxation, during routine ophthalmic evaluation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement appropriate diagnostic investigations, including gonioscopy, anterior segment optical coherence tomography, ultrasound biomicroscopy, and biometry, to evaluate anterior segment anatomy and disease severity.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based management strategies, including refractive correction, laser iridotomy, and surgical options, based on disease stage and complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with optometrists, ophthalmologists, and allied health professionals to ensure timely referral and coordinated interprofessional care.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=141167&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=141167">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Microspherophakia is a rare congenital anomaly characterized by an abnormally spherical crystalline lens&#x000a0;with increased anteroposterior thickness and reduced equatorial diameter.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r1">[1]</xref> The primary pathology lies in the development of zonules. The condition is often bilateral and is characterized by small, thick, and spherical lenses. A mutation in the&#x000a0;latent transforming&#x000a0;growth factor beta-binding protein 2&#x000a0;(<italic toggle="yes">LTBP2</italic>) gene is often implicated as the underlying pathology. The most common associations include lens subluxation, anterior or posterior dislocation, high myopia, accommodation deficits, or raised intraocular pressure (IOP).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Rare cases of microspherophakia associated with retinitis pigmentosa, Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome, and XYY syndrome have also been reported.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Microspherophakia represents a distinct entity within the spectrum of developmental lens anomalies, with significant implications for both refractive status and anterior segment biomechanics. The spherical configuration of the lens increases its refractive power, predisposing affected individuals to lenticular myopia, which may be progressive and often disproportionate to axial length. This optical abnormality is frequently among the earliest clinical manifestations and may serve as an important diagnostic clue in pediatric and young adult populations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Anatomically, the abnormal lens morphology is closely linked to defective zonular architecture, which may be hypoplastic, elongated, or abnormally inserted. This zonular weakness not only contributes to the spherical configuration but also predisposes the lens to instability, resulting in phacodonesis, subluxation, or complete dislocation. The direction of dislocation may vary with the residual zonular support and may involve anterior migration into the anterior chamber or posterior displacement into the vitreous cavity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>A critical aspect of microspherophakia is its association with secondary glaucoma, which significantly influences long-term visual prognosis. The mechanism of&#x000a0;IOP elevation is multifactorial, including pupillary block due to an anteriorly displaced spherical lens, crowding of the anterior chamber angle, and progressive angle closure. Recurrent episodes of angle closure may lead to peripheral anterior synechiae formation and chronic glaucomatous optic neuropathy if not addressed promptly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>The condition is frequently associated with systemic syndromes, most notably Weill-Marchesani syndrome, which is characterized by short stature, brachydactyly, joint stiffness, and ocular findings including microspherophakia and ectopia lentis. Other systemic associations include variants of Marfan syndrome, homocystinuria, and connective tissue disorders, although phenotypic overlap varies widely. Recognition of these associations is essential, as they may necessitate multidisciplinary evaluation and management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Genetically, mutations in&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">LTBP2</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">ADAMTS10</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">ADAMTS17</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">FBN1</italic> genes have been implicated in the pathogenesis of microspherophakia. These genes are involved in extracellular matrix integrity and zonular fiber development, explaining the structural abnormalities observed in the lens and its suspensory apparatus. Advances in molecular genetics have improved the understanding of genotype-phenotype correlations, although significant heterogeneity remains.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Clinically, microspherophakia often presents during childhood or adolescence, but milder cases may remain undiagnosed until adulthood. Patients may present with decreased vision, fluctuating refractive error, or acute symptoms related to lens dislocation or angle-closure glaucoma. Slit-lamp examination typically reveals a small, spherical lens with increased curvature, often best appreciated after pharmacologic dilation. Gonioscopy may demonstrate narrow or occludable angles, particularly in advanced cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;See <bold>Image.</bold> Microspherophakia in Marfan syndrome.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The natural history of microspherophakia is variable. In some patients, the condition remains stable with manageable refractive error, whereas in others, progressive zonular weakness leads to increasing lens instability and complications. The risk of acute angle-closure glaucoma remains a lifelong concern and may occur unpredictably, necessitating close monitoring. In addition to structural and refractive abnormalities, microspherophakia can significantly impact visual development, particularly in pediatric patients. Uncorrected lenticular myopia and associated amblyopia may lead to long-term visual deficits if left untreated early. Therefore, timely diagnosis and appropriate refractive correction are essential components of management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Recent advances in imaging modalities such as anterior segment optical coherence tomography (AS-OCT) and ultrasound biomicroscopy (UBM) have enhanced the ability to evaluate lens morphology, zonular integrity, and anterior chamber configuration in microspherophakia. These tools provide valuable information for both diagnosis and surgical planning, particularly in complex cases with associated glaucoma or lens instability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Overall, microspherophakia is a complex condition with significant variability in presentation and outcomes. The management of microspherophakia&#x000a0;requires a comprehensive understanding of its anatomical, genetic, and clinical features, along with a multidisciplinary approach tailored to the individual patient. Early recognition and appropriate intervention are critical to preventing irreversible visual loss and optimizing long-term prognosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The underlying etiology lies in the development of zonules during embryogenesis. Genetic mutations in the latent <italic toggle="yes">LTBP2</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">ADAMTS17</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">FBN1</italic> genes have also been implicated. The <italic toggle="yes">LTBP2</italic> gene is expressed in the trabecular meshwork, ciliary processes, and lens capsule, and its encoded protein shows structural homology to fibrillin proteins. This gene is responsible for the assembly of elastic fibers. <italic toggle="yes">ADAMTS17</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">FBN1</italic> genes are the primary genes implicated in Weill-Marchesani syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;See <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Etiology of Microspherophakia.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Microspherophakia is fundamentally a disorder of zonular development and extracellular matrix organization, leading to abnormal lens morphology and biomechanics. During embryogenesis, the zonules&#x02014;derived from the non-pigmented ciliary epithelium&#x02014;play a crucial role in maintaining the equatorial tension of the crystalline lens. Any disruption in their formation, structure, or anchorage reduces the lens's centrifugal force, allowing it to assume a more spherical configuration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>The <italic toggle="yes">LTBP2</italic> gene is critical in maintaining extracellular matrix integrity and microfibril stability. Mutations in this gene impair the anchoring of zonular fibers to the lens capsule and ciliary body, leading to zonular laxity or absence, a key feature of microspherophakia. Additionally, its expression in the trabecular meshwork suggests a potential role in the pathogenesis of secondary glaucoma, linking structural abnormalities to dysfunction of the aqueous outflow pathway.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>The ADAMTS family (a&#x000a0;disintegrin and metalloproteinase with thrombospondin motifs), particularly ADAMTS10 and ADAMTS17, is involved in the processing and assembly of extracellular matrix proteins, including fibrillin-rich microfibrils. Dysfunction of these enzymes leads to defective microfibrillar architecture, resulting in weak or fragmented zonules and altered lens biomechanics. These abnormalities are particularly evident in Weill-Marchesani syndrome, where microspherophakia is a hallmark ocular feature.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;See <bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Microspherophakia in Weill-Marchesani syndrome.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The <italic toggle="yes">FBN1</italic> gene, encoding fibrillin-1, is another key component of zonular fibers. Mutations in <italic toggle="yes">FBN1</italic> disrupt microfibril formation and stability, leading to a spectrum of connective tissue disorders, including Marfan syndrome and its variants.&#x000a0;Although Marfan syndrome is more commonly associated with ectopia lentis due to zonular stretching, certain mutations may present with microspherophakia due to altered zonular tension dynamics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>In addition to genetic causes, evidence suggests that non-genetic or idiopathic forms of microspherophakia may occur, possibly due to localized developmental anomalies affecting zonular differentiation. These cases often lack systemic associations and may present as isolated ocular findings.</p>
        <p>The interplay between these genetic and developmental factors ultimately results in:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Reduced zonular tension</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Increased anteroposterior lens thickness</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Decreased equatorial diameter</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens instability and predisposition to dislocation</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These structural alterations not only define the morphology of microspherophakia but also explain its clinical sequelae, including myopia, pupillary block, and secondary glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-141167.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
