      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Microspherophakia is often associated with other developmental anomalies. This&#x000a0;association has also been well recognized as part of syndromic associations, such as Weill-Marchesani syndrome. The prevalence of Weill-Marchesani syndrome has been estimated at 1&#x000a0;in 100,000. Although isolated microspherophakia has been identified across different ethnic groups, its prevalence has not been extensively studied. A study from India reported a prevalence of 1.2% for microspherophakia among children presenting to the clinic with lens abnormalities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Microspherophakia predominantly presents in pediatric and adolescent populations, with most cases diagnosed during the first or second decade of life, when refractive errors or lens instability become clinically apparent. Unlike age-related lenticular conditions, it does not exhibit a degenerative pattern but rather reflects an early developmental anomaly. No consistent gender predilection has been observed, although some case series suggest a slight male predominance in syndromic forms, likely reflecting inheritance patterns rather than true biological susceptibility. The absence of large population-based datasets, however, limits the ability to draw definitive conclusions regarding sex distribution.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>From a geographic perspective, microspherophakia has been reported across diverse populations, but higher reporting frequency from South Asia and the Middle East may reflect increased clinical recognition, genetic clustering, or referral bias in tertiary ophthalmic centers. Regions with a higher prevalence of consanguinity may show an increased incidence of autosomal recessive forms, particularly those associated with&#x000a0;mutations&#x000a0;in the&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">ADAMTS</italic> gene. Syndromic microspherophakia constitutes a significant proportion of reported cases. Among these, Weill-Marchesani syndrome remains the most consistently associated systemic condition, followed by occasional associations with connective tissue and developmental disorders. In contrast, isolated microspherophakia is likely underdiagnosed, especially in mild cases where refractive error is the only presenting feature.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;See <bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Epidemiology of Microspherophakia.</p>
        <p>Another important epidemiological consideration is the risk of secondary glaucoma, which may develop in a substantial subset of patients over time. Studies suggest that glaucoma may occur in up to 30% to 50% of affected individuals, particularly in those with significant anterior segment crowding or lens subluxation. This finding highlights the need for long-term surveillance even in asymptomatic patients. Delayed diagnosis remains a critical issue, especially in resource-limited settings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients often present only after complications such as acute angle closure or lens dislocation, suggesting that the true prevalence of microspherophakia may be underestimated in community settings. With the increasing availability of advanced anterior segment imaging (AS-OCT and UBM) and genetic testing, there is a growing recognition of subclinical or early-stage disease. This may lead to a future rise in reported prevalence as diagnostic sensitivity improves.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r12">[12]</xref></p>
