      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The crystalline lens is almost spherical at birth, with an anteroposterior thickness of 3.5 mm and an equatorial diameter of 6.5 mm. These dimensions gradually increase to a thickness of 5 mm and to equatorial dimensions of approximately 9 to 10 mm by adulthood. In the presence of weak zonules, reduced tension at the equatorial plane results in a more spherical, convex lens. This lens shape leads to high lenticular myopia and often to subluxation due to weak zonules.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>The 2 primary pathophysiological mechanisms implicated in microspherophakia are as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>During the fifth to sixth decade of embryonic life, delayed formation or defective insertion of secondary lenticular fibers may occur, often resulting from malnutrition and defects in the tunica vasculosa lentis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Due to abnormal development of lenticular fibers, the zonules may become rudimentary, and the lens may remain spherical rather than its normal biconvex shape.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r1">[1]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The altered lens geometry increases refractive power due to steep curvature, resulting in disproportionate lenticular myopia independent of axial length. This optical change is often dynamic and may fluctuate with subtle shifts in zonular tension. Anterior displacement of the spherical lens contributes to shallow anterior chamber depth and crowding of the iridocorneal angle, predisposing to intermittent or chronic pupillary block and secondary angle-closure glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>Biomechanically, reduced zonular support increases lens mobility (phacodonesis), which may progress to partial or complete lens dislocation with minimal stress or physiological changes. At the molecular level, abnormalities in extracellular matrix proteins disrupt microfibrillar architecture and zonular elasticity, thereby impairing the transmission of ciliary body forces required to maintain normal lens contour and accommodation dynamics. Over time, these combined structural and functional alterations result in a spectrum of clinical manifestations, including progressive refractive error, angle compromise, and instability of the lens-iris diaphragm, which form the core pathophysiological basis of microspherophakia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;See <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Pathophysiology of Microspherophakia.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Pathophysiology Takeaways</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Core defect &#x02192; Zonular weakness + altered lens biomechanics</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optical effect &#x02192; High lenticular myopia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mechanical effect &#x02192; Lens instability + subluxation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anterior segment effect &#x02192; Pupillary block + glaucoma risk</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Histopathological analysis has revealed that the zonules can be rudimentary and unusually long. Moreover, developmental defects and an abnormal distribution of secondary lenticular fibers may be present. Eventually, it can result in hyaloid degeneration due to changes in lenticular shape that disrupt the lenticular fibers. Microscopically, the crystalline lens in microspherophakia demonstrates increased cortical compaction, with lens fibers appearing densely packed due to altered spatial orientation rather than increased cellular proliferation. This&#x000a0;structural arrangement contributes to the increased anteroposterior thickness observed clinically. The lens epithelial cells may exhibit subtle morphological and organizational abnormalities, particularly in the equatorial region where lens fiber differentiation occurs. Disruption in this process results in irregular fiber elongation and disordered layering, affecting the structural integrity of the lens.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;See<bold> Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Histopathology of Microspherophakia.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>At the level of the lens capsule, variations in thickness and elasticity may be observed, reflecting underlying abnormalities in the extracellular matrix. These changes can impair the lens's normal biomechanical response to zonular tension and ciliary body forces. The zonular fibers, when examined ultrastructurally, often exhibit fragmentation, reduced density, and disorganized microfibrillar architecture. These fibers are primarily composed of fibrillin-rich microfibrils, and defects in their assembly compromise tensile strength and anchoring capacity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>Electron microscopy studies have demonstrated abnormal extracellular matrix deposition, with irregular microfibril alignment and altered interfibrillar spacing. This structural disorganization weakens the connection between the lens capsule and ciliary processes. In advanced cases, chronic biomechanical instability may lead to secondary degenerative changes, including localized lens fiber disruption, vacuole formation, and early opacification. These findings may precede clinically significant cataract formation. Additionally, changes in the surrounding ocular structures may be observed, including alterations in the trabecular meshwork involving extracellular matrix remodeling that may contribute to impaired aqueous outflow in patients with secondary glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r26">[26]</xref></p>
