        <p>
<bold>Key Histopathology Insights</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The core defect lies in the microfibrillar and zonular architecture</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens changes are due to abnormal organization, not hyperplasia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ultrastructural abnormalities explain clinical instability and shape</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Secondary degenerative changes may precede clinical complications&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r27">[27]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s7" sec-type="Toxicokinetics">
        <title>Toxicokinetics</title>
        <p>Although microspherophakia is primarily a developmental and genetic disorder, toxicokinetic mechanisms may be indirectly relevant via ionic imbalances and biochemical alterations that affect lens metabolism and extracellular matrix stability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>The crystalline lens maintains its transparency and structural integrity through tightly regulated ionic homeostasis, particularly involving sodium (Na<sup>+</sup>), potassium (K<sup>+</sup>), and calcium (Ca<sup>2+</sup>) ions. Disruption of zonular architecture and altered lens biomechanics may impair permeability of the lens fiber cell membrane, leading to dysregulated ionic gradients and intracellular fluid accumulation. Elevated intracellular calcium levels can activate calcium-dependent proteolytic enzymes (calpains), resulting in the degradation of lens proteins and cytoskeletal elements. This process contributes to lens fiber disorganization and early degenerative changes, which may predispose to opacification in advanced cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Additionally, abnormalities in extracellular matrix proteins, such as fibrillin and LTBP2, may alter cell-matrix signaling pathways, indirectly influencing ionic transport mechanisms and metabolic activity in lens epithelial cells. These changes can affect cellular differentiation and maintenance of lens architecture. Oxidative stress may further exacerbate these processes by impairing ion pump function (Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase) and promoting the accumulation of reactive oxygen species, leading to progressive cellular dysfunction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Overall, although not a primary toxic disorder, microspherophakia involves secondary ionic and biochemical dysregulation that contributes to lens instability, altered refractive properties, and long-term structural changes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Points</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Not a classical toxic disease &#x02192; Secondary toxicokinetic relevance</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Core issue &#x02192; Ionic imbalance (Ca<sup>2+</sup>&#x000a0;and Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> disruption)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Consequences include:</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Protein degradation</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Fiber disorganization</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Lens instability</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Oxidative stress worsens cellular dysfunction&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s8" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Microspherophakia is often bilateral, and patients commonly present with complaints of low vision and defective accommodation. Rarely,&#x000a0;they may complain of acute pain and redness&#x000a0;due to acute angle closure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r28">[28]</xref> Some patients report uniocular diplopia due to lens subluxation. In children, occasional symptoms such as flashes and floaters may occur, and untreated cases can lead to amblyopia. Fundoscopy may reveal signs of pathological myopia, such as a myopic disc, myopic crescent, posterior staphyloma, and myopic macular degeneration. Additional findings may include blue sclera, ectopic pupil, retinal detachment, tears, retinal holes, or lattice degeneration.</p>
        <p>In a study by Muralidhar et al, all patients demonstrated varying&#x000a0;degrees of lenticular myopia, with a mean refractive error of &#x02212;11.07 dioptres. Almost 45% developed glaucoma, and half of these had high&#x000a0;IOP at presentation. The risk of developing high IOP and angle-closure glaucoma increases if the lens is displaced anteriorly. Microspherophakia is often an isolated finding or may be associated with syndromes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;Homocystinuria is the most commonly reported systemic association.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;See <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Microspherophakia in Homocystinuria.</p>
        <p>Microspherophakia may be associated with both systemic and local conditions. The systemic associations include Lowe syndrome, Marfan syndrome, Weill-Marchesani syndrome, Alport syndrome, microspherophakia-metaphyseal dysplasia, Cri du chat syndrome, Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome, congenital rubella, Peter anomaly, hyperlysinaemia, Klinefelter syndrome, chondrodysplasia punctata, and metaphyseal dysplasia. Local associations include aniridia, megalocornea, optic disc colobomata, and iridocorneal endothelial syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>A more detailed history often reveals that&#x000a0;visual complaints in microspherophakia are&#x000a0;progressive rather than sudden, particularly when lenticular myopia gradually increases. Patients or caregivers may report frequent changes in spectacle prescription that are&#x000a0;disproportionate to age-related refractive trends. In children, this may manifest as poor scholastic performance, difficulty with near tasks, or head-tilting behaviors, suggesting uncorrected refractive error or accommodative strain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>Episodes of intermittent ocular discomfort, halos around lights, or transient blurring of vision may indicate subclinical or intermittent angle closure, especially in patients with anterior lens displacement. A history of sudden, painful visual loss with associated redness and photophobia should raise suspicion for acute angle closure, which may be the first presentation in undiagnosed individuals. Patients with lens instability may report fluctuating vision with&#x000a0;gaze position, attributable to subtle shifts in lens position. In cases of posterior dislocation, symptoms may include floaters, visual distortion, or decreased contrast sensitivity, whereas anterior dislocation may cause acute corneal edema and marked visual reduction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>A&#x000a0;detailed systemic history is essential, as microspherophakia may be part of a broader syndromic spectrum. Inquiry into growth patterns, joint mobility, cardiovascular history, and developmental milestones can provide critical clues toward associated systemic disorders. Family history should also be explored, particularly in populations with consanguinity, as this may suggest autosomal recessive inheritance. On slit-lamp biomicroscopy, careful attention should be paid to the contour and position of the crystalline lens. The lens often appears more convex with a reduced equatorial diameter, and after pharmacologic dilation, the lens equator may be visualized within the pupillary zone, which is a highly suggestive finding. The anterior chamber is typically shallow and uniform, although asymmetry may be present in cases of subluxation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Assessment of lens stability is critical and can be performed by observing for phacodonesis during ocular movements. Even minimal zonular weakness can produce subtle tremulousness that might otherwise go unnoticed&#x000a0;without dynamic examination. In advanced cases, visible lens decentration may occur, either anteriorly or posteriorly.&#x000a0;Evaluation of the anterior chamber angle by gonioscopy is essential, as structural crowding from the spherical lens predisposes to angle narrowing and eventual closure. The degree of angle compromise may not always correlate with symptoms, underscoring the importance of this screening tool even in asymptomatic patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>Measurement of IOP should be interpreted cautiously, as values may fluctuate depending on lens position and angle status. Serial measurements are often more informative than a single reading, particularly in patients with intermittent symptoms. Posterior segment examination provides valuable insight into the chronic effects of refractive changes. Findings such as tilted optic discs, peripapillary atrophy, and posterior staphyloma reflect long-standing myopic stress, even in the absence of significant axial elongation. In cases with advanced complications, retinal breaks or detachment may be identified and require prompt intervention. In pediatric patients, assessment should also include evaluation for amblyopia and binocular function, as early visual deprivation or anisometropia may lead to long-term deficits if not addressed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Flowchart: Clinical Approach to Microspherophakia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patient with&#x000a0;reduced&#x000a0;vision&#x000a0;or high myopia&#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;&#x000a0; Refraction &#x02192; Disproportionate lenticular myopia&#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;&#x000a0; Slit-lamp&#x000a0;examination &#x02192; Spherical lens &#x000b1; phacodonesis&#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;&#x000a0; Dilated examination&#x000a0;&#x02192; Lens equator visibility&#x000a0;or subluxation&#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;&#x000a0; Gonioscopy &#x02192; Narrow or occludable angles&#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;&#x000a0; IOP Monitoring &#x02192; Detect secondary glaucoma&#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;&#x000a0; Fundus Evaluation &#x02192; Myopic or retinal changes&#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;&#x000a0; Systemic Workup &#x02192; Syndromic associations&#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x02193;&#x000a0; Diagnosis &#x02192; Microspherophakia &#x000b1; complications&#x000a0;</p>
