              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="width: 123px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cardiovascular</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 221.406px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Aortic abnormalities</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 215.594px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Marfan&#x000a0;syndrome</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="width: 123px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Metabolic</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 221.406px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Neurological features</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 215.594px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Homocystinuria</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="width: 123px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Facial</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 221.406px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Dysmorphic features</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 215.594px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Genetic syndromes</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s9" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The ocular examination should include a detailed slit-lamp examination. Key parameters include retinoscopy, best-corrected visual acuity, IOP, anterior chamber angle and depth, gonioscopy, lens morphology, and signs of subluxation. Dilated fundus examination is needed to look for glaucomatous disc changes, myopic retinal changes, or coexisting retinal pathology. Gupta et al reported an E sign for the early clinical recognition of microspherophakia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>Extra investigations might include lens thickness, axial length, UBM, and AS-OCT. These investigations may help understand the mechanism of angle closure in these patients. The role of iTrace has been described in assessing the underlying cause of preoperative reduced visual acuity and in predicting postoperative outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>Often, patients have a reduced endothelial cell count, and electron microscopy may be needed before planning surgical intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r35">[35]</xref> Apart from these, a detailed systemic examination is essential to rule out any syndromic features. A comprehensive evaluation of microspherophakia requires the integration of anterior segment imaging, biometric analysis, and functional assessment to fully characterize the anatomical and physiological abnormalities.</p>
        <p>Advanced imaging modalities, such as Scheimpflug imaging (Pentacam), provide quantitative assessment of anterior chamber depth, lens vault, and anterior segment configuration, allowing early identification of angle crowding before clinical gonioscopic closure becomes apparent. This modality is particularly useful in detecting subtle anatomical predispositions to angle closure. AS-OCT enables high-resolution, non-contact imaging of the iridocorneal angle and lens position, facilitating dynamic assessment of angle behavior under different lighting conditions.<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>This modality is especially valuable in pediatric or uncooperative patients, where gonioscopy may be challenging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>UBM remains the gold standard for evaluating zonular integrity and lens position, particularly in cases of suspected subluxation or posterior displacement. This modality allows visualization of structures posterior to the iris, including ciliary body-zonule-lens relationships, which are critical in understanding the mechanism of pupillary block. Biometric evaluation using optical biometry (IOL Master or Lenstar) helps differentiate lenticular myopia from axial myopia by documenting axial length, anterior chamber depth, and lens thickness. Increased lens thickness with relatively normal axial length is highly suggestive of microspherophakia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Functional assessment using wavefront aberrometry and ray-tracing systems (eg, iTrace) provides insight into higher-order aberrations and internal optics, helping to quantify the contribution of lenticular abnormalities to visual impairment. This&#x000a0;type of functional assessment is particularly useful in preoperative planning and predicting postoperative visual outcomes. Specular microscopy should be performed to evaluate corneal endothelial cell density, especially in cases with anterior lens subluxation or chronic angle crowding, where endothelial compromise may influence surgical decision-making. In suspected syndromic cases, genetic evaluation and molecular testing may be considered to identify mutations in <italic toggle="yes">LTBP2</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">ADAMTS</italic>, or <italic toggle="yes">FBN1</italic> genes, particularly in young patients or those with systemic associations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;See <bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Clinical Evaluation of Microspherophakia.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-141167.table6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
