        <p>
<bold>Schematic Diagram: Evaluation Pathway</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patient with reduced&#x000a0;vision&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;high myopia&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x02193;Basic clinical examination(visual acuity, refraction, slit lamp, IOP)&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x02193;Suspicion of lens abnormality&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x02193;Anterior segment assessment(gonioscopy + AC depth evaluation)&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x02193;Advanced imaging (AS-OCT&#x000a0;or UBM)&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x02193;Biometry (axial length + lens thickness)&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x02193;Functional assessment (iTrace&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;aberrometry)&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x02193;Systemic + genetic evaluation (if needed)&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x02193;Final diagnosis + risk stratification</p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical Integration of Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <p>The evaluation of microspherophakia is not limited to structural identification; it also includes&#x000a0;risk stratification, particularly for glaucoma and lens instability. Early identification of angle compromise allows prophylactic or therapeutic intervention before irreversible optic nerve damage occurs. Similarly, understanding the contribution of lenticular abnormalities to refractive error helps plan appropriate visual rehabilitation strategies. The integration of imaging, clinical examination, and systemic evaluation ensures a holistic diagnostic approach, minimizing missed diagnoses and improving long-term outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r36">[36]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s10" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The specific management depends on the presenting features. A targeted management protocol includes the following:</p>
        <p>
<bold>Lenticular Myopia</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>All patients should be evaluated for underlying refractive errors. Timely correction of refractive errors helps prevent irreversible vision loss and amblyopia. Regular follow-up is essential to monitor refractive changes and adjust correction accordingly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r37">[37]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Pupillary Block&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pupillary block&#x000a0;is a common complication due to the lens's spherical shape. Prophylactic Nd:YAG laser iridotomy can help prevent pupillary block glaucoma. An acute attack can be controlled with cycloplegics. Cycloplegics relax the ciliary muscles, thereby tightening the zonules and pulling the lens-iris diaphragm posteriorly. Intravenous mannitol also helps by causing the vitreous to shrink and thus relieving the pupillary block.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Angle-Closure Glaucoma </bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients may achieve IOP control following YAG iridotomy. Some patients may need additional antiglaucoma medications to control the IOP. Advanced cases may require glaucoma surgery, such as a filtration procedure or a glaucoma drainage device, to further lower IOP and prevent additional damage to the disc.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;Senthil et al reported success rates of up to 86% at 6 months after trabeculectomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r8">[8]</xref> Goel et al reported successful management of 2 sisters with bilateral microspherophakia and advanced angle-closure glaucoma using combined procedures, including lensectomy, vitrectomy, and transversal ciliary body photocoagulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r39">[39]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Subluxated Lens </bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The spherical lens with a smaller equatorial diameter and weak zonules is prone to anterior or posterior subluxation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;These cases may be planned for a precise lens extraction. IOL placement, if possible, should be targeted within the bag. Additionally, capsular tension rings or segments may be used, if needed. Few novel modifications, such as the double-flanged technique, in which capsulorhexis is performed with a femtosecond laser followed by standard capsular tension ring insertion, have been described for the management of microspherophakia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r41">[41]</xref> Clear lens extraction and goniosynechiolysis, with or without secondary IOL implantation via a sclerally fixed anterior or pars plana route, can be planned in patients with corneal-lenticular touch or posterior dislocation into the vitreous cavity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;In patients with high myopia,&#x000a0;lens extraction alone may be appropriate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r43">[43]</xref><bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Indications for clear lens extraction in patients with microspherophakia include corneal-lenticular touch, high myopia in one eye, and pupillary block glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Low Vision</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients often have low best-corrected visual acuity due to high lenticular myopia and refractive amblyopia, and thus need low-vision assistive devices. Low vision, in the form of reduced best-corrected visual acuity or restricted visual fields, can also result from advanced glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r44">[44]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Systemic Conditions </bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluation by a clinician is essential to rule out any systemic associations. Multidisciplinary management involving ophthalmologists, optometrists, orthopedists, and cardiologists may be required for comprehensive care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r21">[21]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s11" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The clinical conditions linked to microspherophakia include:</p>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Ocular Associations </bold></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Aniridia&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Megalocornea</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Iridocorneal endothelial syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>High lenticular myopia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Disc coloboma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Retinal detachment&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r45">[45]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Systemic Associations </bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Alport syndrome&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r46">[46]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Weill-Marchesani syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Homocystinuria</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Marfan syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Metaphyseal dysplasia</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-141167.table8" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 9. Differential Diagnosis of Microspherophakia and Related Conditions</title>
          </caption>
