      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s12" sec-type="Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials">
        <title>Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials</title>
        <p>The current evidence base for microspherophakia is largely derived from retrospective case series, observational studies, and small cohort analyses, owing to the condition's rarity. Large randomized controlled trials are limited; however, existing studies provide important insights into disease progression, glaucoma risk, and surgical outcomes. One of the most clinically significant findings across studies is the high incidence of secondary glaucoma, reported in approximately 40% to 50% of affected eyes. In a longitudinal clinical study, nearly half of the patients developed elevated IOP, with a substantial proportion presenting with glaucoma at initial diagnosis. These findings underscore the importance of early detection and continuous monitoring, as glaucomatous damage may progress despite medical therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Surgical management has been extensively evaluated, particularly the role of lensectomy in controlling both refractive error and IOP. Multiple case series have demonstrated that removal of the abnormally shaped crystalline lens results in significant improvement in visual acuity and reduction in lenticular myopia, along with partial control of IOP. In a recent observational series, postoperative visual acuity improved markedly, and IOP decreased, with reduced dependence on antiglaucoma medications, following lens extraction combined with intraocular lens (IOL) implantation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>However, the effect of lensectomy on glaucoma control remains variable. Some studies suggest that while visual outcomes improve, IOP may remain elevated in a subset of patients, indicating that glaucoma in microspherophakia may involve additional mechanisms beyond pupillary block, including trabecular dysfunction and chronic angle changes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>Recent literature has also focused on advances in IOL fixation techniques, including scleral-fixated and iris-claw lenses. These approaches have shown promising results in improving postoperative stability and visual rehabilitation, particularly in patients with significant zonular weakness, in whom capsular support is inadequate. Long-term follow-up studies indicate that such techniques can provide stable visual outcomes with acceptable complication rates, although careful patient selection remains critical.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Emerging research has proposed novel classification systems for microspherophakia based on severity, lens position, and glaucoma status. These classification models aim to improve clinical decision-making and guide treatment strategies. Early results suggest that risk stratification based on anatomical and functional parameters may help predict surgical outcomes and long-term prognosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>From a diagnostic perspective, studies have highlighted the role of advanced anterior segment imaging, including AS-OCT and UBM, in understanding the mechanism of angle closure and lens dynamics. These modalities have improved the ability to detect subclinical disease and guide timely intervention, particularly in pediatric populations. Despite these advancements, a significant gap in high-level evidence persists due to the condition's rarity. Current research trends emphasize the need for multicentric registries, prospective cohort studies, and standardized treatment protocols to define optimal management strategies and long-term outcomes better.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r23">[23]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s13" sec-type="Treatment Planning ">
        <title>Treatment Planning </title>
        <p>Management of microspherophakia requires a stage-wise, individualized approach based on 3 key determinants:refractive status, lens stability, and presence of glaucoma or angle compromise. The treatment plan should be dynamic, evolving with disease progression and patient age.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Initial Clinical Stratification</bold>
</p>
        <p>At presentation, patients should be categorized into:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Stable lens without glaucoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens instability (subluxation&#x000a0;or dislocation)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Associated glaucoma or angle closure</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>This classification guides the urgency and modality of intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r48">[48]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Conservative Management Strategy</bold>
</p>
        <p>In early or uncomplicated cases, treatment focuses on visual rehabilitation and surveillance. Accurate refractive correction using spectacles or contact lenses is essential, particularly in pediatric patients, to prevent amblyopia. Regular follow-up is critical to monitor for progressive lens instability or angle narrowing, which may necessitate escalation of care.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Glaucoma-Oriented Planning</bold>
</p>
        <p>When IOP elevation or angle compromise is identified, management should initially focus on medical control of IOP, including aqueous suppressants. However, given the anatomical basis of glaucoma in microspherophakia, definitive management often requires addressing the lens component.</p>
        <p>Laser peripheral iridotomy may be considered in selected cases with a pupillary block mechanism, although its effectiveness may be limited due to persistent lens-induced crowding. Continuous monitoring is necessary, as glaucoma may progress despite initial medical control.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r49">[49]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Decision-Making</bold>
</p>
        <p>Surgical intervention is indicated in the presence of:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Progressive or significant lens subluxation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visually significant lenticular myopia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uncontrolled or high-risk glaucoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recurrent episodes of angle closure</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The primary surgical approach involves lens extraction (lensectomy or phacoaspiration), which addresses both refractive error and anatomical crowding.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Intraocular Lens Strategy</bold>
</p>
        <p>IOL implantation should be tailored based on zonular support:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Adequate capsular support &#x02192; In-the-bag or sulcus IOL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Compromised zonules &#x02192; Scleral-fixated or iris-claw IOL</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pediatric&#x000a0;or complex cases &#x02192; Aphakia with secondary IOL</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The choice of technique should prioritize long-term stability and a minimal risk of complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r50">[50]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Combined and Adjunctive Procedures</bold>
</p>
        <p>In patients with coexisting glaucoma, the following combined procedures may be considered based on disease severity and surgeon expertise.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens extraction with trabeculectomy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens extraction with minimally invasive glaucoma surgery</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Long-Term Management Strategy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Post-treatment care should focus on:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitoring for IOP fluctuations and glaucoma progression</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Assessing visual rehabilitation outcomes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Detecting retinal complications or late lens-related issues</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Patients require lifelong follow-up due to the chronic and progressive nature of the condition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r52">[52]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Treatment Planning Flow</bold>
</p>
        <p>Diagnosed microspherophakia &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193; Assess:</p>
        <p>(Lens stability + refraction + IOP) &#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Stable +&#x000a0;no glaucoma &#x02192; conservative Rx&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x02193;Lens instability&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;high myopia &#x02192; lens surgery&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x02193;Glaucoma present &#x02192; medical &#x000b1; surgical&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x02193; Final goal:</p>
        <p>Stable vision + controlled IOP + anatomical Restoration</p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical Integration</bold>
</p>
        <p>Treatment planning in microspherophakia is centered on early identification of high-risk features, timely surgical intervention when indicated, and prevention of irreversible complications such as glaucoma and amblyopia. A tailored approach ensures optimal visual and anatomical outcomes while minimizing long-term morbidity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s14" sec-type="Toxicity and Adverse Effect Management">
        <title>Toxicity and Adverse Effect Management</title>
        <p>Management of microspherophakia involves both medical and surgical interventions, each with potential adverse effects that require careful anticipation, monitoring, and timely management. Given the chronic nature of the condition and the frequent need for long-term follow-up, minimizing treatment-related morbidity is an integral part of care.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Medical Therapy&#x02013;Related Toxicity</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients receiving antiglaucoma medications may develop ocular surface toxicity, particularly with long-term use of topical agents containing preservatives. Manifestations include dry eye symptoms, conjunctival hyperemia, or superficial punctate keratopathy, which can affect compliance and visual quality. Switching to preservative-free formulations or reducing medication burden following definitive treatment may help mitigate these effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r48">[48]</xref></p>
        <p>Systemic adverse effects must also be considered, especially in pediatric or elderly populations. Beta-blockers may cause cardiopulmonary adverse effects, whereas carbonic anhydrase inhibitors may cause metabolic disturbances or paresthesias. Careful drug selection and coordination with clinicians are essential in such cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r53">[53]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Laser and Procedural Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Laser peripheral iridotomy, when performed for pupillary block, may be associated with transient spikes in IOP, inflammation, or glare symptoms. In microspherophakia, anatomical crowding may reduce the long-term effectiveness of laser procedures, necessitating close monitoring for recurrent angle closure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Complications and Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Lens extraction procedures in microspherophakia are technically challenging due to zonular weakness and capsular instability, thereby increasing the risk of intraoperative complications, including zonular dialysis, capsular rupture, or vitreous loss. Preoperative planning, using appropriate surgical techniques and devices (eg, capsular tension rings or hooks), is crucial to reduce these risks.</p>
        <p>Postoperatively, patients may develop inflammation, elevated IOP, cystoid macular edema, or retinal complications, particularly in cases with preexisting myopic changes or abnormalities at the vitreoretinal interface. Early identification and prompt management of these complications are essential to preserve visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r54">[54]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Intraocular Lens&#x02013;Related Adverse Effects</bold>
</p>
        <p>In cases requiring alternative fixation techniques, such as scleral-fixated or iris-claw IOLs, complications may include IOL decentration, dislocation, pigment dispersion, or endothelial cell loss. Regular postoperative evaluation of lens position and corneal status is necessary, especially in younger patients with long life expectancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r55">[55]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Long-Term Complication Mitigation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Chronic angle compromise and fluctuating intraocular pressure may lead to progressive glaucomatous damage despite initial control. Therefore, long-term management should focus on regular IOP monitoring, optic nerve assessment, and visual field evaluation.</p>
        <p>Additionally, patients should be monitored for late-onset retinal complications, particularly in those with associated myopic changes or prior surgical intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r56">[56]</xref></p>
