        <p>
<bold>Clinical Integration</bold>
</p>
        <p>Effective toxicity management in microspherophakia requires a proactive, preventive approach that&#x000a0;balances&#x000a0;the benefits of intervention against potential risks. Tailored therapy, vigilant follow-up, and early recognition of complications are essential to optimize long-term visual and anatomical outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s15" sec-type="Staging">
        <title>Staging</title>
        <p>Microspherophakia&#x000a0;lacks a universally standardized staging system; however, for clinical practice and management planning, the condition can be stratified based on&#x000a0;lens&#x000a0;morphology, degree of zonular compromise, anterior segment configuration, and the&#x000a0;presence of complications such as glaucoma. A structured staging approach helps predict disease progression, guide interventions, and optimize visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Proposed Clinical Staging System</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Stage I: Early&#x000a0;or Subclinical Disease</bold>
</p>
        <p>In the initial stage, patients typically present with mild lenticular myopia without significant anatomical distortion of the anterior segment. The crystalline lens remains centrally located with minimal or no evidence of instability. The anterior chamber depth is within normal or borderline limits, and intraocular pressure is typically normal. Diagnosis at this stage often occurs incidentally during routine examination or refractive assessment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r57">[57]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Stage II: Established Structural Abnormality</bold>
</p>
        <p>A well-defined spherical lens morphology, increased anteroposterior thickness, and early zonular weakness characterize this stage. Patients may demonstrate phacodonesis or subtle lens subluxation, along with a relatively shallow anterior chamber. Although intraocular pressure may still be within normal limits,&#x000a0;the risk of&#x000a0;angle narrowing is increasing, making this stage critical for close monitoring and early intervention planning.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r58">[58]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Stage III: Functional Compromise with Angle Involvement</bold>
</p>
        <p>At this stage, progressive anterior displacement of the lens leads to significant anterior chamber crowding and occludable angles. Patients may present with intermittent symptoms of angle closure, including pain, halos, or transient visual disturbances. Intraocular pressure may begin to rise, either intermittently or persistently. Structural instability of the lens becomes more pronounced, increasing the likelihood of subluxation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r59">[59]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Stage IV: Advanced Disease with Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Advanced microspherophakia is characterized by frank lens subluxation or dislocation, anterior or posterior, often accompanied by secondary glaucoma. Chronic angle closure, optic nerve damage, and visual field defects may be present. Additional complications such as retinal detachment, corneal endothelial compromise, or cataractous changes may also develop. Visual prognosis becomes guarded at this stage, and surgical intervention is often required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r59">[59]</xref></p>
