        <p>
<bold>Clinical Application of Staging</bold>
</p>
        <p>This staging system allows clinicians to adopt a proactive rather than reactive approach, identifying patients at risk of progression before irreversible complications arise. Early-stage disease primarily requires monitoring and refractive correction, whereas advanced stages necessitate surgical planning and glaucoma management. Importantly, progression between stages is not always linear and may be influenced by factors such as genetic background, zonular integrity, and patient age. Therefore, periodic reassessment is essential to detect subtle changes that may alter the management strategy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s16" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Prognosis depends on the severity of the presentation. In a study by Senthil et al, 51% of patients had glaucoma in eyes with microspherophakia. Success rates following trabeculectomy were reported as 86% at 6 months and 77% at 1 year. These results were the same until 7 years into the follow-up period. Blindness was reported in up to 20% of eyes at presentation and up to 30% at the last follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>In another study, Senthil et al reported outcomes following trabeculectomy in 29 eyes of 18 patients with microspherophakia, with a probability of complete success of 96% at 1 year and 88% at 2 years of follow-up. These results were maintained over a 7-year follow-up period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r60">[60]</xref>&#x000a0;Harasymowycz&#x000a0;et al reported a case of trabeculectomy in one eye that required lensectomy, vitrectomy with a scleral-fixed IOL, and a Molteno tube shunt implant to manage a persistent flat anterior chamber following trabeculectomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r61">[61]</xref></p>
        <p>Another study by Garudadri et al on 61 eyes with microspherophakia reported that 12 eyes required laser iridotomy and 40 required surgical intervention, including trabeculectomy, lensectomy, or drainage procedures. Twenty eyes (16%) were blind due to glaucoma.</p>
        <p>The long-term visual prognosis in microspherophakia is influenced not only by the presence of glaucoma but also by the timing of diagnosis and intervention. Patients identified in earlier stages, before significant anterior segment compromise, generally achieve better outcomes due to timely control of refractive error and prevention of secondary complications. In contrast, delayed presentation is often associated with irreversible optic nerve damage and structural changes that limit visual recovery even with&#x000a0;appropriate management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>The degree of lens instability also plays a crucial role in determining prognosis. Eyes with minimal zonular weakness tend to maintain stable visual acuity over time with conservative management. In contrast, progressive subluxation or dislocation significantly increases the risk of secondary complications, including corneal endothelial damage, vitreoretinal traction, and recurrent angle-closure episodes. These factors contribute to a more guarded prognosis in advanced disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Refractive outcomes are generally favorable following appropriate correction or surgical intervention; however, variability exists, particularly in pediatric patients, depending on the presence of&#x000a0;coexisting amblyopia. Early optical rehabilitation is therefore essential to optimize visual development and prevent permanent visual deficits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>The presence of chronic angle compromise is a key determinant of visual prognosis. Even when intraocular pressure is initially controlled, long-term outcomes depend on sustained IOP stability and preservation of optic nerve function. Progressive glaucomatous damage remains one of the most significant causes of visual loss in these patients, highlighting the importance of continuous monitoring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Several factors, including zonular integrity, anterior chamber anatomy, and surgeon experience, influence surgical prognosis. Eyes with relatively preserved zonular support tend to have better anatomical and refractive outcomes following lens extraction. In contrast, those with extensive zonular deficiency may require complex surgical approaches, increasing the risk of postoperative complications and variable visual recovery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Additionally, patients with syndromic associations may have a more complex disease course due to multisystem involvement, which can indirectly affect ocular prognosis. For instance, systemic connective tissue disorders may predispose to progressive zonular weakening or to altered wound-healing responses, impacting long-term stability.</p>
        <p>The risk of retinal complications, although less common, must also be considered, particularly in eyes with longstanding myopic changes or vitreous alterations. Such complications can further compromise vision and may require additional interventions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r62">[62]</xref></p>
        <p>Overall, the prognosis of microspherophakia is best described as variable but potentially favorable with early detection and appropriate management. At the same time, advanced disease with glaucoma and structural complications carries a significantly poorer outlook.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r23">[23]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s17" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Microspherophakia can be associated with complications secondary to&#x000a0;the biconvex lens shape, which can result in high myopia. Furthermore, a relatively weak zonule lens is prone to subluxation or even complete anterior or posterior dislocation. Anterior subluxation can lead to corneal-lenticular contact, thereby predisposing to endothelial cell loss or endothelial decompensation.</p>
        <p>Posterior lens dislocation can lead to vitreous traction and subsequent retinal detachment. Earlier studies have reported an incidence of up to 44% of lens subluxation in patients with microspherophakia. Furthermore, anterior lens subluxation can lead to a shallow anterior chamber and pupillary block, or the patient may present with intermittent iridolenticular touch.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>The incidence of angle-closure glaucoma has been reported as high as 44.4% to 51%. Chronic pupillary block glaucoma can result from the formation of peripheral anterior synechiae, trabecular crowding by the spherophakic lens, reverse angle-closure glaucoma, or malignant glaucoma. Rarely, non-resolving malignant glaucoma may result in permanent vision loss or phthisis, and, in some cases, a painful blind eye requiring enucleation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r63">[63]</xref></p>
        <p>Beyond lens-related instability and glaucoma, microspherophakia may lead to a spectrum of secondary complications in the anterior and posterior segments, particularly in long-standing or untreated cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Anterior Segment Complications</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic irido-lenticular contact may lead to persistent inflammation and contribute to the development of posterior synechiae, thereby further exacerbating pupillary block mechanisms.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prolonged anterior chamber shallowing may predispose to chronic endothelial stress, eventually leading to progressive endothelial dysfunction even in the absence of direct lens touch.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Repeated episodes of subclinical angle closure may lead to trabecular meshwork damage, reducing outflow facility and contributing to refractory glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In some cases, prolonged inflammation and anatomical distortion may lead to&#x000a0;secondary cataractous changes, particularly after&#x000a0;recurrent lens instability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r12">[12]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Posterior Segment Complications</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic vitreous traction from posteriorly dislocated lenses may lead not only to retinal detachment but also to vitreoretinal interface abnormalities, including vitreous degeneration and tractional changes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Long-standing refractive error and altered ocular biomechanics may predispose to progressive myopic degeneration, even in the absence of significant axial elongation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rarely, repeated mechanical stress may contribute to macular changes, including early foveal thinning or distortion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r62">[62]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical and Post-intervention Complications</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgical management in microspherophakia carries an increased risk of zonular dialysis, capsular instability, and vitreous prolapse, due to inherent structural weakness.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Postoperative complications may include cystoid macular edema, persistent inflammation, or secondary glaucoma progression, particularly in eyes with preexisting angle compromise.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In cases requiring alternative IOL fixation, the risk of IOL tilt, decentration, or long-term instability may affect visual quality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r23">[23]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Pediatric and Developmental Complications</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>In children, delayed diagnosis and inadequate refractive correction may lead to amblyopia, which can significantly limit visual outcomes even with later intervention.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Binocular vision abnormalities, including reduced stereopsis, may develop if anisometropia or visual axis obstruction is present for prolonged periods.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r6">[6]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Chronic Disease&#x02013;Related Complications</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Progressive disease may result in recurrent angle-closure episodes, leading to cumulative optic nerve damage over time.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Long-standing cases may develop mixed-mechanism glaucoma, where both pupillary block and trabecular dysfunction coexist.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In severe, untreated cases, persistent inflammation and elevated intraocular pressure may culminate in&#x000a0;phthisis bulbi, a painful, blind eye that requires palliative management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s18" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>Postoperative care includes short-term topical steroids and cycloplegics, with or without antiglaucoma medications. Patients should be followed up for refractive error and corrected with glasses for residual refraction or aphakia. Intraocular pressure must be monitored at every visit for at least 6 months. Patients with low best-corrected visual acuity or restricted fields due to glaucomatous disc damage may need low-vision assistive devices, and appropriate counseling on lifestyle modifications should be emphasized.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>In the postoperative period, meticulous attention to anterior segment stability and control of inflammation is essential, particularly in eyes with preexisting zonular weakness or angle compromise. Early postoperative assessment should focus on wound integrity, anterior chamber depth, and IOL position, as subtle shifts in anatomy may influence both visual recovery and intraocular pressure dynamics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Visual rehabilitation should be approached in a structured manner, with early optical correction tailored to the refractive status and surgical outcome. In aphakic or pseudophakic patients, timely prescription of appropriate visual aids is critical for optimizing functional vision, especially in younger individuals, whose visual development is ongoing. Periodic reassessment is necessary to address refractive fluctuations that may occur during the healing phase.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Long-term follow-up should emphasize glaucoma surveillance, including optic nerve evaluation and functional testing where feasible. Even in cases with initially stable intraocular pressure, delayed elevation may occur due to evolving changes in the angle or trabecular dysfunction. Therefore, monitoring should extend beyond the early postoperative window into long-term care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Rehabilitation also includes the evaluation of binocular vision and visual function, particularly in pediatric patients, in whom amblyopia therapy or orthoptic interventions may be required. In selected cases, referral to low vision services can enhance quality of life by providing customized assistive strategies for patients with irreversible visual impairment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r64">[64]</xref></p>
        <p>Patient education forms an integral component of postoperative care, with emphasis on recognizing symptoms of complications such as pain, sudden visual decline, or photophobia. Counseling on adherence to follow-up schedules and medication use improves long-term outcomes and reduces the risk of preventable vision loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s19" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Any child presenting to the outpatient department with complaints of defective vision, pain, and redness should undergo a detailed evaluation to identify the underlying ocular pathology. All patients with a probable diagnosis of microspherophakia must be referred to a pediatric ophthalmologist for meticulous evaluation and targeted treatment to prevent irreversible vision-threatening sequelae and amblyopia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r65">[65]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Glaucoma specialist:&#x000a0;Referral is essential in patients with elevated intraocular pressure, narrow angles, or suspected angle-closure mechanisms to guide long-term IOP control and surgical planning.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Vitreoretinal specialist:&#x000a0;Indicated in cases with posterior lens dislocation, vitreous traction, or associated retinal pathology such as lattice degeneration, retinal tears, or detachment.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cornea specialist:&#x000a0;Consultation is warranted when evidence of endothelial compromise, corneal edema, or corneo-lenticular touch is present, particularly in advanced anterior subluxation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Geneticist&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;clinical genetic counseling:&#x000a0;Recommended in patients with suspected syndromic associations (eg, Weill-Marchesani syndrome, Marfan syndrome, and homocystinuria) for confirmation of diagnosis, family screening, and prognostic counseling.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pediatrician&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;physician:&#x000a0;Necessary for systemic evaluation, especially in children, to identify associated metabolic or connective tissue disorders and coordinate multidisciplinary care.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Low vision rehabilitation specialist:&#x000a0;For patients with reduced visual potential due to amblyopia or glaucomatous damage, to optimize functional vision and improve quality of life.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Orthoptist&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;vision therapy specialist:&#x000a0;Particularly important in pediatric patients for the management of amblyopia, binocular dysfunction, and optimization of visual development.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anesthesiologist (preoperative assessment):&#x000a0;Required in surgical candidates, especially pediatric or syndromic patients, to evaluate systemic risks and plan safe perioperative care.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s20" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>All patients must be educated regarding the need for regular follow-up.&#x000a0;They&#x000a0;should be advised to seek urgent medical care if they develop acute redness, pain, colored halos, or sudden vision deterioration. Patients&#x000a0;should be reminded of the need for timely screening for refractive error and intraocular pressure, and to have their glasses adjusted as needed. Surgical intervention may be planned based on the assessment at subsequent visits. Patient counseling is essential to ensure understanding that microspherophakia has a favorable prognosis and that good visual acuity can be achieved with timely intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Effective patient education should extend beyond routine counseling to include a clear understanding of the progressive nature of the condition and the risk of late complications, particularly glaucoma. Patients and caregivers should be informed that, even in the absence of symptoms, anatomical changes within the eye may continue to evolve, necessitating periodic reassessment. This need for pediatric patients is especially important, as early detection of changes can significantly influence long-term visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Education should also focus on recognizing subtle visual symptoms, such as intermittent blurring or visual fluctuations, which may indicate early lens instability or angle compromise. Prompt reporting of such symptoms can facilitate early intervention and prevent irreversible damage. Lifestyle guidance plays an important supportive role. Patients should be advised to avoid activities that may cause sudden increases in intraocular pressure or trauma, particularly in cases of known lens instability. Protective measures, including the use of appropriate eyewear during high-risk activities, may help reduce the risk of lens displacement or secondary complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r64">[64]</xref></p>
        <p>In individuals with associated systemic conditions, counseling should include information about the disease's multisystem nature and encourage coordinated care with other specialists. Family members may also benefit from awareness and, when appropriate, screening, particularly in hereditary cases. Long-term adherence to follow-up and treatment recommendations is crucial. Establishing a structured follow-up schedule and ensuring patients understand its importance can significantly reduce the likelihood of advanced disease presentation. Continuous reinforcement of these educational points during each visit helps improve compliance and overall outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s21" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Microspherophakia is a clinical diagnosis, and all patients with this condition should be aggressively treated to achieve an excellent visual outcome. A detailed systemic evaluation is mandatory in all cases to rule out syndromic associations. Special emphasis should be given to children to prevent amblyopia and secondary glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Early recognition of microspherophakia is crucial, as subtle clinical signs such as disproportionate lenticular myopia or a shallow anterior chamber may precede overt complications. A high index of suspicion is required, particularly in young patients presenting with unexplained high myopia and normal axial length. Timely diagnosis allows for appropriate monitoring and intervention before irreversible damage occurs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>A key clinical consideration is that the disease course may remain asymptomatic until complications develop, especially glaucoma. Therefore, reliance on symptoms alone may delay diagnosis, emphasizing the importance of comprehensive ocular examination, including gonioscopy and anterior segment evaluation, even in apparently stable patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r62">[62]</xref></p>
        <p>Surgical decision-making requires careful planning, as zonular weakness complicates intraocular procedures. Anticipating intraoperative challenges and appropriately selecting surgical techniques are essential to avoid complications and ensure optimal outcomes. Another important aspect is the variability in clinical presentation, which may range from isolated lens abnormalities to complex syndromic cases. This&#x000a0;variability necessitates an individualized management approach that integrates ocular findings with systemic evaluation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical Pearls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>High lenticular myopia with a normal axial length should always prompt evaluation for underlying lens abnormalities.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A shallow anterior chamber in a young patient is an important warning sign for potential angle-closure mechanisms.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gonioscopy should be performed routinely, even in asymptomatic cases, to detect early angle closure.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early optical correction in pediatric patients is essential to prevent amblyopia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r22">[22]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Common Pitfalls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Misdiagnosing lenticular myopia as axial myopia, leading to delayed recognition of the underlying condition.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Underestimating the risk of glaucoma in asymptomatic patients.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delayed referral for specialist evaluation, particularly in pediatric cases.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inadequate preoperative assessment of zonular integrity before surgical intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r66">[66]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Prevention and Risk Reduction</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Regular screening of at-risk individuals, particularly those with syndromic associations or family history.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early identification and management of refractive errors to prevent amblyopia.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Periodic monitoring of intraocular pressure and anterior chamber angle status.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Patient and caregiver education regarding warning symptoms and the need for long-term follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r31">[31]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Disposition and Follow-up</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients with microspherophakia require long-term, often lifelong follow-up, with periodic reassessment of visual acuity, intraocular pressure, and anterior segment anatomy. The frequency of follow-up should be individualized based on disease severity, presence of glaucoma, and risk of progression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-141167.s22" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Patients presenting with high myopia, acute angle-closure glaucoma, or spontaneously subluxated lenses should be carefully evaluated to rule out microspherophakia. Optometrists are often the first point of contact for these patients. All individuals presenting with high myopia for the first time should be referred to an ophthalmologist for a comprehensive dilated anterior and posterior segment examination. The nursing team can help explain the need for regular follow-ups, prepare patients for any surgical intervention, and guide patients on postoperative medications and regular follow-up care. They also play a vital role in providing cross-references to low-vision clinics and in guiding patients with lifestyle modifications and overall psychological well-being.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>Assessment by a pediatrician or clinician is necessary to rule out and manage any systemic associations. Thus, an interprofessional, holistic management approach can help achieve maximum success and optimal visual outcomes. Effective management of microspherophakia relies on seamless interprofessional communication and early coordinated care pathways. Timely identification by primary eye care providers, followed by structured referral systems, ensures that patients receive specialized evaluation before complications develop. Establishing clear referral protocols among optometrists, general ophthalmologists, and subspecialists improves diagnostic accuracy and reduces management delays.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Ophthalmologists play a central role in clinical decision-making and longitudinal care, including risk stratification for glaucoma, surgical planning, and postoperative monitoring. Subspecialists such as glaucoma and vitreoretinal surgeons contribute to the management of complex cases, particularly those with advanced disease or posterior segment involvement. Advanced practitioners and physician assistants support continuity of care by facilitating patient education, adherence to follow-up appointments, and the coordination of investigations, ensuring that no aspect of care is fragmented. Their role is particularly valuable in high-volume clinical settings where consistent patient tracking is required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r62">[62]</xref></p>
        <p>Nursing professionals are integral to patient safety, especially in perioperative settings. They ensure proper medication administration, early recognition of complications, and reinforcement of postoperative instructions, thereby improving compliance and reducing preventable adverse outcomes. Their involvement in counseling also helps address patient anxiety and improves engagement with long-term care plans.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r67">[67]</xref></p>
        <p>Pharmacists contribute by optimizing medication safety and adherence, particularly in patients requiring long-term antiglaucoma therapy. They assist in identifying potential drug interactions, minimizing systemic side effects, and recommending preservative-free alternatives when ocular surface toxicity is a concern.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r68">[68]</xref></p>
        <p>From an ethical perspective, shared decision-making is essential, especially when considering surgical intervention in pediatric or complex cases. Patients and caregivers should be actively involved in understanding the risks, benefits, and long-term implications of treatment options. Maintaining transparency and ensuring informed consent enhances trust and improves overall care satisfaction. Coordinated care also extends to systemic evaluation, in which collaboration with pediatricians, geneticists, and other specialists enables a comprehensive assessment of syndromic associations. This multidisciplinary approach ensures that both ocular and systemic aspects of the condition are addressed effectively. Ultimately, optimizing healthcare team outcomes in microspherophakia requires a structured, patient-centered care model that emphasizes early detection, coordinated interventions, and sustained follow-up. Such an approach not only improves visual outcomes but also enhances patient safety, treatment adherence, and overall quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-141167.r69">[69]</xref></p>
      </sec>
